# The history carries five distinct author identities. Four of them are the
# same person under different names, machines, or typos:
#
#   43  muneebexotic <muneebusman112233@gmail.com>      correct
#   33  Muneeb Usman <muneebusman112233@gmail.com>      right email, other name
#   21  Muneeb U. <...@users.noreply.github.com>        commits made on github.com
#   15  muneebexotic3 <musman@innovative-pk.com>        a work account, wrong entirely
#    1  Muneeb Usman <muneebusma112233@gmail.com>       typo: "muneebusma", missing an n
#
# The `musman@innovative-pk.com` commits came from this machine's GLOBAL git
# identity, which belongs to a different account. The single typo'd address
# (commit 2036c90, 2025-07-11) is a plain slip that has been sitting in the
# history for a year.
#
# A mailmap fixes attribution for `git log --use-mailmap`, `git blame` and
# `git shortlog` **without rewriting anything**. Two things it does not do,
# stated so it is not mistaken for more than it is:
#
#   1. GitHub links a commit to an account by the raw author email, so its
#      web UI keeps showing the wrong ones until history is actually
#      rewritten.
#   2. It does not prevent a wrong identity being used again. The pre-commit
#      hook does that.
#
# To make it permanent, SECURITY-REMEDIATION.md §2.5 passes `--mailmap` to
# git-filter-repo during the history purge that is already planned. The
# rewrite is happening regardless, so this correction rides along for free.
#
# Format:  <canonical name> <canonical email> <commit name> <commit email>

